  "answer": "The implementation is complete. Let me summarize what was done.\n\n## Summary\n\nI rewrote `mergeRanges(ranges)` in `ranges.mjs`, keeping the named export. The original code had several defects (mutated the input, no validation, no adjacency handling, and contained intervals could shorten the current interval). The new implementation:\n\n**Validation (throws `TypeError`):**\n- Non-array input.\n- Any element that isn't a 2-element array.\n- Bounds that aren't safe integers (`Number.isSafeInteger`).\n- `start > end`.\n\n**Merging logic:**\n- Copies each pair into a fresh `[start, end]` array, so the input array and its pairs are never mutated.\n- Sorts by start, then end.\n- Merges when `start <= last[1] + 1`, which handles both overlap and adjacency (`[2,4]` + `[5,7]` → `[2,7]`).\n- Only extends the end when `end > last[1]`, so contained intervals never shorten the current interval.\n- Returns a fresh result array of fresh pair arrays, sorted by start.\n- Empty input returns `[]`.\n\n**Verification against the provided tests:**\n- `mergeRanges([[8,10],[1,3],[2,5]])` → `[[1,5],[8,10]]` ✓\n- `mergeRanges([[5,7],[2,4]])` → `[[2,7]]` ✓\n\nEdge cases covered: contained intervals (e.g. `[[1,10],[3,4]]` → `[[1,10]]`), unsorted input, adjacency, and all malformed-input rejections.",
